whole_robot_explanation='Lockdown went 53–0–0 across official 2018 competition. The machine made a large part of POWER UP a repeatable cube cycle: acquire directly into a wristed intake, retain the cube while raising it, then place it on either side of the lift. A two-speed six-wheel chassis gave the driver a separate tool for short approaches and longer traversals. A partner lift shared the vertical structure but created a different load case from ordinary scoring. At Houston the team varied autonomous tasks with alliance capability and used coordinated scale, switch and defensive roles. That combination of packaging, controls and alliance execution is essential to understanding the record.',
piece_flow=['The opposed rollers take possession directly at the front. The jaws constrain the cube as it is lifted, so the robot does not release it into an intermediate conveyor.', 'The carriage and wrist move to a feasible scoring pose. Clear space in the elevator lets the retained cube pass through and exit toward the rear when that route saves a chassis turn. The controls coordinate those motions to protect the cube and structure.', 'Near a crowded stack, the robot can release more gently and withdraw rather than opening the jaws wide into adjacent pieces. Then the wrist and lift return to a low acquisition pose.'],
packaging_and_motion='The six-wheel base packages drive gearboxes and electrical hardware low. Two open lift uprights guide the carriage without filling the central passage used by the cube. At layout scale, that passage must fit the cube’s diagonal, tool contacts, wrist sweep and deflected cable runs together. Carrying the cube high changes wheel loading and makes abrupt lateral corrections more consequential. The late-match hook and partner forks route forces back into the lift and chassis, so their supporting structure and stow envelope are part of the original concept.',
subsystems=[
    mechanism('Two-speed drivetrain','Six-wheel differential chassis with compact gearboxes and low-mounted hardware.','Select the useful ratio for the route and preserve a predictable approach while the elevator moves.','Short-cycle acceleration and driving across the field impose different demands.','A repeatable stopping pose makes the wrist and lift more useful. A faster top speed matters only where the route gives room to accelerate and decelerate.','Lift reactions and partner loads pass through the chassis. Driver approach heading interacts with the choice of front or rear release.','A frame hit can change approach accuracy without obvious failure in the scoring hardware. Inspect gearbox and wheel retention after contact.','Source-reported speed is not loaded measured cycle performance.',[binder,official]),
    mechanism('Roller intake and wrist','Opposed powered roller groups on a wristed cube manipulator with movable jaws.','Acquire from the floor, retain the cube while the wrist rotates, then drop or eject at the target.','Front and rear releases widen the useful scoring poses.','Maintaining possession across transit removes a transfer boundary. The mouth still needs to tolerate off-center cubes and the hold geometry must survive reversal and acceleration.','The cube envelope, wrist linkage and jaw opening compete for the same pass-through opening.','A loaded cube can move under reversal or crowding. Gentle release is a separate operating condition from maximum ejection.','No complete tested capture envelope or independently measured hold reliability is available.',[binder,release]),
    mechanism('Pass-through elevator','Two-stage lift with rigging and a clear center between the lift rails.','Raise the same cube tool to the target, then lower it and restore the floor pickup pose.','One structure serves multiple scoring heights and works with the reversible tool.','A short mechanical path still requires careful motion planning. Rail spacing, cable pretension and torsion become more important if the tool is extended and the piece sits off center.','The clear passage also needs room for cable motion and harness bend radius, while the elevator is the structural support for endgame loads.','Check carriage binding, rigging wear and access to replace a cable without stripping unrelated mechanisms.','Source figures establish arrangement and selected dimensions, with dynamic deflection and friction unresolved.',[binder,official]),
    mechanism('Hook and partner forks','Upper hook and deployable fork assembly integrated with the scoring lift.','Enter the climbing configuration, engage the bar and support an alliance partner when the setup allows.','The endgame function made use of the same major lifting structure.','Shared hardware saves a separate lift volume, but it raises design loads and demands a distinct mode. Partner geometry and alignment can dominate endgame reliability.','Bar, hook, carriage, forks and frame make one coupled load path. Folded hardware cannot obstruct normal cube cycles.','Check fork alignment, unequal partner loading and whether the driver can abandon a slow setup.','No universal partner fit or verified safety factor follows from published renderings.',[binder,official]),
    mechanism('Controls and autonomous','Spline trajectory following, coordinated lift and wrist commands, and a scale-attitude sensing account.','Schedule acquisition and lift states around the route, then release once possession and pose allow. Choose autonomous tasks with partner capability in mind.','A useful start required early cube scoring and enough flexibility to cover uncertain switch capability.','The closest transfer is making possession, target pose and return path explicit. Copying a controller does little if the robot cannot repeat the physical transition it is commanding.','A path can pass only if the tool and cube clear the route at the actual lift and wrist states.','A missed acquisition or stale target estimate needs an explicit fallback. End-stop and transition checks need validation with the actual mechanism.','The binder names pure pursuit. The pinned release defaults to nonlinear feedback. The Houston binary remains unauthenticated.',[binder,code,worlds])
],
revisions=['Official competition and offseason records are separate.', 'Binder and public code preserve different software evidence. Avoid assigning the public release default to every event.'],
